<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>This chapter focuses on merge sort, quicksort, and counting sort. There =
is additional information on the other algorithms in the references provide=
d, as well as a tool for visualizing how these algorithms work <a href=3D"h=
ttps://www.toptal.com/developers/sorting-algorithms" target=3D"_blank" rel=
=3D"noopener noreferrer">[7]</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Fundamental concepts for sorting algorithms</strong><br>
<!-- -->Here=E2=80=99s a list of fundamental attributes of sorting algorith=
ms you should be familiar with:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Stability:</strong> A sorting algorithm is considered stable if =
it preserves the relative order of equal elements in the sorted output. If =
two elements have equal values, their order in the sorted output is the sam=
e as in the input.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>In-place sorting:</strong> An in-place sorting algorithm transfo=
rms the input using a constant amount of extra storage space. It involves s=
orting the elements within the original data structure.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Comparison-based sorting:</strong> Comparison-based sorting algo=
rithms sort elements by comparing them pairwise. These algorithms typically=
 have a lower bound of <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><=
math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</m=
i><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mo>=E2=81=A1</mo><mo =
stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo><mo stretch=
y=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(n\log=
(n))</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-=
hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:=
1em;vertical-align:-0.25em"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D=
"margin-right:0.02778em">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=
=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0=
.1667em"></span><span class=3D"mop">lo<span style=3D"margin-right:0.01389em=
">g</span></span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord mathnorm=
al">n</span><span class=3D"mclose">))</span></span></span></span>, whereas =
non-comparison-based sorting algorithms can achieve linear time complexity,=
 but require specific assumptions about the input data.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>These concepts are referenced throughout the problems in this chapter.</=
p>
<h2 id=3D"real-world-example">Real-world Example</h2>
<p><strong>Sorting products by category:</strong> When users search for pro=
ducts, the platform often sorts the results based on various criteria such =
as lowest to highest price, highest to lowest rating, or even relevance to =
the search query. Efficient sorting algorithms ensure large datasets of pro=
ducts can be quickly arranged according to user preferences.</p>
